00:20:24.336 --> 00:20:25.900
API key security.

00:20:25.900 --> 00:20:29.170
So we didn't get to
generate an API key.

00:20:29.170 --> 00:20:32.620
But when you do generate an API
key, the default security on it

00:20:32.620 --> 00:20:35.860
is none, which is OK.

00:20:35.860 --> 00:20:38.240
Especially if you're
just messing around,

00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:40.210
if you're using it
within your organization,

00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:43.480
if you're making
requests to analyze data,

00:20:43.480 --> 00:20:46.240
you're probably not going to
have anyone steal your key.

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:50.856
In my time at Google, I think
I've seen this happen one time.

00:20:50.856 --> 00:20:52.480
And it really doesn't
matter that much,

00:20:52.480 --> 00:20:54.760
because you can
create a new project

00:20:54.760 --> 00:20:56.250
and you can create a new key.

00:20:56.250 --> 00:20:57.967
But it can happen.

00:20:57.967 --> 00:20:59.800
And like I said, your
key is your signature.

00:20:59.800 --> 00:21:02.926
So think of it like you
see these weird charges

00:21:02.926 --> 00:21:04.300
on your credit
card or something.

00:21:04.300 --> 00:21:06.466
You're like oh, I should
probably cancel this credit

00:21:06.466 --> 00:21:07.750
card and get a new one.

00:21:07.750 --> 00:21:09.850
Your API key is
something that it

00:21:09.850 --> 00:21:12.460
is a best practice to secure.

00:21:12.460 --> 00:21:13.820
We love security at Google.

00:21:13.820 --> 00:21:17.830
So we do give you opportunity
to secure your API key.

00:21:17.830 --> 00:21:19.600
When you create a
key, like I mentioned,

00:21:19.600 --> 00:21:22.654
in the credential section
of the developers console,

00:21:22.654 --> 00:21:23.820
you can name it if you want.

00:21:23.820 --> 00:21:27.700
So I named it Emily's
super secure key.

00:21:27.700 --> 00:21:31.360
And you have the opportunity
to restrict it to HTTP refer.

00:21:31.360 --> 00:21:34.870
So for example, if
I'm building my Emily

00:21:34.870 --> 00:21:37.750
likes donuts.com, where
users can actually

00:21:37.750 --> 00:21:39.940
go order donuts from my shop.

00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:42.820
And I plan on using the
JavaScript API, which

00:21:42.820 --> 00:21:46.660
is one of our web APIs in
that website to build a Store

00:21:46.660 --> 00:21:48.950
Locator, I might want
to restrict this key

00:21:48.950 --> 00:21:53.890
to only being able to be used
from Emily like donuts.com.

00:21:53.890 --> 00:21:57.790
And that way my competitor
Bethany Likes Donuts.com

00:21:57.790 --> 00:22:01.810
can't find my key and my front
end code and start using it,

00:22:01.810 --> 00:22:08.680
and using up my free quota
as a standard plan developer.

00:22:08.680 --> 00:22:11.850
I can also restrict
it to IP addresses.

00:22:11.850 --> 00:22:14.860
So for example, my server,
if I'm doing a data analysis,

00:22:14.860 --> 00:22:17.310
and I want to restrict it
to certain IP addresses.

00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:20.200
And then I can also
restrict it to Android apps

00:22:20.200 --> 00:22:23.610
and iOS apps using
those app IDs.

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:28.030
So this is definitely a best
practice that we'd recommend.

00:22:28.030 --> 00:22:29.030
Rate limiting.

00:22:29.030 --> 00:22:31.295
This is something that
comes up all the time.

00:22:31.295 --> 00:22:33.170
Rate limiting in
general is a principle

00:22:33.170 --> 00:22:35.930
that is existing in a
lot of different APIs.

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:38.360
It's essentially just
limiting the number

00:22:38.360 --> 00:22:42.860
of requests that you can make
in a certain number of time

00:22:42.860 --> 00:22:45.130
to each API.

00:22:45.130 --> 00:22:47.190
For the Google Maps
APIs, in general

00:22:47.190 --> 00:22:50.400
that's queries per second.

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:53.890
In some of the APIs, such
as the Distance Matrix API,

00:22:53.890 --> 00:22:55.600
it can get a little
bit more complicated.

00:22:55.600 --> 00:22:58.710
So when you make a request
to the Distance Matrix API,

00:22:58.710 --> 00:23:02.490
you actually specify multiple
origins and destinations

00:23:02.490 --> 00:23:04.950
that you might be going to.

00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:06.990
And so in that
situation, it's actually

00:23:06.990 --> 00:23:10.830
kind of based on number of
things you request per second.

00:23:10.830 --> 00:23:12.960
And you can find more
details on that kind of thing

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:14.820
in our developer documentation.

00:23:14.820 --> 00:23:18.420
We'll just in general talk
about queries per second.

00:23:18.420 --> 00:23:21.360
And it's 50 as a
default right now.

00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:24.960
And so businesses say all the
time why do you have this?

00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:27.090
If you want to be
infinitely scalable,

00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:29.850
why do we have to adhere
to this kind of thing?

00:23:29.850 --> 00:23:32.940
And our response is
usually let's work together

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:36.570
to figure out what
your expected QPS is.

00:23:36.570 --> 00:23:40.560
But it is really important
to have that expectation.

00:23:40.560 --> 00:23:42.980
Basically, having
that QPS allows

00:23:42.980 --> 00:23:45.860
us to do capacity
planning for one thing.

00:23:45.860 --> 00:23:49.290
But it protects you and
it protects your end users

00:23:49.290 --> 00:23:51.899
against something either--

00:23:51.899 --> 00:23:54.190
I was going to say how many
of you have developed bugs.

00:23:54.190 --> 00:23:56.860
But you're not like super
engaged with the hand raising,

00:23:56.860 --> 00:23:58.318
and I know you
would all lie to me.

00:23:58.318 --> 00:24:01.180
Everybody has developed
a bug at some point.

00:24:01.180 --> 00:24:03.430
And so you don't want
a bug in your system--

00:24:03.430 --> 00:24:05.200
let's say we didn't
have rate limits--

00:24:05.200 --> 00:24:09.100
to use a million queries per
second, and your app goes down,

00:24:09.100 --> 00:24:11.980
and Google server goes down,
and the internet breaks.

00:24:11.980 --> 00:24:15.340
And you're sitting at
your computer like oops.

00:24:15.340 --> 00:24:16.780
So that's kind of
the main thing.

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:18.780
It protects end users
across the board.

00:24:18.780 --> 00:24:20.472
And it protects
your application.

00:24:20.472 --> 00:24:22.180
So if you have billing
enabled, if you're

00:24:22.180 --> 00:24:25.990
an up and running business
and you do pay for some usage,

00:24:25.990 --> 00:24:29.200
and then some kind of bug or
maybe a malicious user that

00:24:29.200 --> 00:24:35.992
has taken your API key
makes a ton of requests,

00:24:35.992 --> 00:24:36.950
we would figure it out.

00:24:36.950 --> 00:24:39.310
We would do all the
Google-y things.

00:24:39.310 --> 00:24:41.080
We would forgive the
usage or something.

00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:44.480
But you get that charge and
you're like what is that?

00:24:44.480 --> 00:24:46.150
So having QPS limits
actually prevents

00:24:46.150 --> 00:24:50.276
some of those harry
situations from happening.

00:24:50.276 --> 00:24:51.900
I'll just leave this
here for a second.

00:24:56.180 --> 00:24:58.680
So this is impossible because
we do have rate limits.

00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:04.140
So you can't use 87 billion
geocodes in five seconds.

00:25:04.140 --> 00:25:05.540
But we don't want
this to happen.

00:25:05.540 --> 00:25:07.290
We don't want you to
spit out your cereal.

00:25:09.830 --> 00:25:11.690
Error handling.

00:25:11.690 --> 00:25:14.640
So again, when we talk
about the client libraries

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:17.390
that you can use to develop
using the Web Service APIs,

00:25:17.390 --> 00:25:19.140
a lot of these things
are handled for you.

00:25:19.140 --> 00:25:21.170
But it's good to
know about them.

00:25:21.170 --> 00:25:24.770
So when you make requests to the
Google Maps web service APIs,

00:25:24.770 --> 00:25:27.880
you get something
called a status code.

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:29.720
And there's a bunch of these.

00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:32.060
They're kind of varied per API.

00:25:32.060 --> 00:25:33.830
Some of them have
unique status codes.

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:35.621
And we're going to talk
about the ones that

00:25:35.621 --> 00:25:38.755
are pretty universal across
all of the Google Maps APIs.

00:25:38.755 --> 00:25:40.755
So there are some statuses
that you want to see.

00:25:40.755 --> 00:25:42.296
This is usually the
first thing you'd

00:25:42.296 --> 00:25:44.660
check for when you get your
response from the APIs.

00:25:44.660 --> 00:25:46.900
An OK status is
what you want it.

00:25:46.900 --> 00:25:47.769
It's thumbs up.

00:25:47.769 --> 00:25:49.310
It means that you
made a request that

00:25:49.310 --> 00:25:52.220
was recognized by our back
end, and we provide you

00:25:52.220 --> 00:25:54.070
with data per your request.

00:25:54.070 --> 00:25:56.080
It's good.

00:25:56.080 --> 00:25:57.999
Zero results is still fine.

00:25:57.999 --> 00:26:00.040
It means that you provided
us with a request that

00:26:00.040 --> 00:26:02.800
was legitimate, and we
didn't find anything.

00:26:02.800 --> 00:26:05.120
And then we told you we
didn't find anything.

00:26:05.120 --> 00:26:08.020
So for example, if
I tried to geocode

00:26:08.020 --> 00:26:13.300
the Flintstones address, which
is 301 Cobblestone Way I think.

00:26:13.300 --> 00:26:15.220
My memory betrays me.

00:26:15.220 --> 00:26:17.890
But I probably would
get zero results,

00:26:17.890 --> 00:26:24.330
or I'd be very surprised
that we found it somewhere.

00:26:24.330 --> 00:26:28.814
So next, statuses where you
want to retry the request.

00:26:28.814 --> 00:26:31.480
Over query limit has a lot to do
with what we just talked about.

00:26:31.480 --> 00:26:34.650
So if you make too many requests
within a certain time period,

00:26:34.650 --> 00:26:38.010
you're going to get this
over query limit response.

00:26:38.010 --> 00:26:40.390
Like I said, usually
this means QPS.

00:26:40.390 --> 00:26:42.240
You've gone over
a certain number

00:26:42.240 --> 00:26:44.280
of queries within a second.

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:46.020
But it can mean queries per day.

00:26:46.020 --> 00:26:50.580
So as a standard plan, which
means basically a free user,

00:26:50.580 --> 00:26:54.390
non enterprise user, you have
a limited number of requests

00:26:54.390 --> 00:26:56.784
that you can make for
free any given day.

00:26:56.784 --> 00:26:58.200
So if you've
exceeded that, you'll

00:26:58.200 --> 00:27:01.637
get this over query
limit response.

00:27:01.637 --> 00:27:03.220
In that situation,
you would basically

00:27:03.220 --> 00:27:05.680
want to retry the request
after a period of time

00:27:05.680 --> 00:27:07.660
using exponential back off.

00:27:07.660 --> 00:27:11.080
Basically you keep retrying
that request over an increasing

00:27:11.080 --> 00:27:14.426
period of time until you
get a successful response.

00:27:14.426 --> 00:27:17.170
For over query limit, if you
don't get a successful response

00:27:17.170 --> 00:27:19.000
after retrying,
it probably means

00:27:19.000 --> 00:27:21.190
you hit that QPD limit,
queries per day limit,

00:27:21.190 --> 00:27:24.540
rather than the queries
per second limit.

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:26.100
Unknown error is
another response

00:27:26.100 --> 00:27:27.780
where you do want
to use exponential

00:27:27.780 --> 00:27:30.600
back off to retry a request.

00:27:30.600 --> 00:27:33.180
Unknown error usually happens
because there was some kind

00:27:33.180 --> 00:27:34.920
of server error on our side.

00:27:34.920 --> 00:27:38.530
Sometimes that happens due to
a high traffic at the time.

00:27:38.530 --> 00:27:40.350
So if you use
exponential back off

00:27:40.350 --> 00:27:43.080
you maybe limit the
number of times you retry.

00:27:43.080 --> 00:27:49.300
But you'll eventually get
an OK response hopefully.

00:27:49.300 --> 00:27:52.020
Finally, we have statuses
where you don't want to retry.

00:27:52.020 --> 00:27:55.170
Something was wrong,
and retrying won't help.

00:27:55.170 --> 00:27:55.920
Request denied.

00:27:55.920 --> 00:27:59.040
So I mentioned API key security.

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:03.060
If Bethany Likes Donuts.com
tries to use my API key,

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:06.090
she's going to get a
request denied error.

00:28:06.090 --> 00:28:09.990
Or if I use an API
key from a server,

00:28:09.990 --> 00:28:12.570
from an IP address that
hasn't been whitelisted,

00:28:12.570 --> 00:28:14.580
like my domain
was whitelisted, I

00:28:14.580 --> 00:28:16.500
would get a request
denied error.

00:28:16.500 --> 00:28:18.930
Similarly, if I try to make
a request to the places API,

00:28:18.930 --> 00:28:22.620
and I don't put an API
key in, it's denied.

00:28:22.620 --> 00:28:24.750
It's basically something
with your credentials

00:28:24.750 --> 00:28:28.930
that is incorrect or
missing in this situation.

00:28:28.930 --> 00:28:31.090
Invalid request is also
something where you don't

00:28:31.090 --> 00:28:32.535
want to retry the request.

00:28:32.535 --> 00:28:33.910
It means that
there was something

00:28:33.910 --> 00:28:35.860
wrong with either
the formatting,

00:28:35.860 --> 00:28:38.030
one of the required
parameters was missing.

00:28:38.030 --> 00:28:44.110
So for example, if I try to geo
code something like a bicycle.

00:28:44.110 --> 00:28:44.860
Not an address.

00:28:44.860 --> 00:28:49.960
If I don't put an address or
a lat long, or a place ID,

00:28:49.960 --> 00:28:52.419
it's going to be like,
what you're talking about?

00:28:52.419 --> 00:28:54.460
So invalid request means
that there was something

00:28:54.460 --> 00:28:55.870
wrong with the request.

00:28:55.870 --> 00:28:57.940
In these situations,
you should log them

00:28:57.940 --> 00:29:01.930
and you should kind of try
to identify the root cause.

00:29:01.930 --> 00:29:03.680
Some of them you can
programmatically fix.

00:29:03.680 --> 00:29:05.429
These are just things
where you definitely

00:29:05.429 --> 00:29:06.632
don't want to retry them.

00:29:09.730 --> 00:29:12.790
There's actually great
video on our developer site.

00:29:12.790 --> 00:29:15.580
I believe it's right on the
landing page for the geocoding

00:29:15.580 --> 00:29:16.480
API.

00:29:16.480 --> 00:29:18.910
But it talks about error
handling and exponential

00:29:18.910 --> 00:29:23.080
back off, specifically in
context of the geocoding API.

00:29:23.080 --> 00:29:25.330
But as mentioned, these
status codes are really pretty

00:29:25.330 --> 00:29:27.420
universal to all of
the web service API*s.

00:29:30.550 --> 00:29:31.300
Mobile apps.

00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:33.460
So when you think
of mobile apps,

00:29:33.460 --> 00:29:37.270
you probably think of developing
in native Android or iOS.

00:29:37.270 --> 00:29:40.990
We do have Android and
iOS SDKs that you can use

00:29:40.990 --> 00:29:43.090
to develop on those platforms.

00:29:43.090 --> 00:29:45.610
But in some situations,
it's better for you

00:29:45.610 --> 00:29:47.920
to use the web service APIs.

00:29:47.920 --> 00:29:49.930
Maybe if there is a
feature or a piece

00:29:49.930 --> 00:29:53.230
of data that's not available
through the native APIs.

00:29:53.230 --> 00:29:55.630
Or if your use case
just lends itself more

00:29:55.630 --> 00:29:57.190
to making web service requests.

00:29:57.190 --> 00:29:59.170
Maybe you want to do
more with the data

00:29:59.170 --> 00:30:02.290
before you return it to
the user's mobile phone.

00:30:02.290 --> 00:30:04.870
We generally don't recommend
embedding your API key

00:30:04.870 --> 00:30:07.990
and making those requests
directly from a mobile app.

00:30:07.990 --> 00:30:11.080
The best practice here is to
proxy those requests through

00:30:11.080 --> 00:30:14.020
a server, and then using
our client libraries--

00:30:14.020 --> 00:30:15.310
which we'll go over soon--

00:30:15.310 --> 00:30:17.230
build a server side
application that

00:30:17.230 --> 00:30:20.410
basically takes in their
request from their mobile phone,

00:30:20.410 --> 00:30:23.334
queries to Google Maps
APIs, gets back the data,

00:30:23.334 --> 00:30:25.000
does whatever you
want with it, and then

00:30:25.000 --> 00:30:27.910
returns it back to the user.

00:30:27.910 --> 00:30:32.250
So as mentioned, the Maps
API share the cloud platform.

00:30:32.250 --> 00:30:35.040
And the best way to do
this is through App Engine,

00:30:35.040 --> 00:30:36.120
Compute Engine.

00:30:36.120 --> 00:30:39.210
It allows you to really use
those services in the most

00:30:39.210 --> 00:30:40.300
integrated way possible.

00:30:43.595 --> 00:30:44.970
Now these famed
client libraries.

00:30:44.970 --> 00:30:45.761
We're finally here.

00:30:45.761 --> 00:30:47.460
I'm giving you the TI 89.

00:30:47.460 --> 00:30:49.230
No more long division.

00:30:49.230 --> 00:30:51.570
Most of the things that
we just talked about

00:30:51.570 --> 00:30:55.060
are handled through
these client libraries.

00:30:55.060 --> 00:30:57.000
So these are open sourced.

00:30:57.000 --> 00:30:58.230
They're on GitHub.

00:30:58.230 --> 00:31:01.830
You can use them to develop
that server application

00:31:01.830 --> 00:31:06.210
to either do the heavy duty
data analysis, leverage location

00:31:06.210 --> 00:31:09.330
intelligence, or to build the
server side application that's

00:31:09.330 --> 00:31:13.120
going to take in those mobile
app requests and proxy them.

00:31:13.120 --> 00:31:15.730
They wrap the functionality
of the Maps APIs.

00:31:15.730 --> 00:31:17.847
And so they're
consistently updated

00:31:17.847 --> 00:31:19.680
as we come out with new
features in the APIs

00:31:19.680 --> 00:31:21.570
to include that functionality.

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:24.340
And as mentioned, they implement
those best practices for you.

00:31:24.340 --> 00:31:26.877
So for example, rate
limiting is handled inside

00:31:26.877 --> 00:31:27.960
of those client libraries.

00:31:27.960 --> 00:31:32.080
And you can set specific
QPS's that you want to use.

00:31:32.080 --> 00:31:35.980
They're available in multiple
languages, which is great for--

00:31:35.980 --> 00:31:40.120
probably developers here
are like those quants

00:31:40.120 --> 00:31:41.950
who's developed in 14
different languages.

00:31:41.950 --> 00:31:45.700
But I'm certainly not
so I appreciate the fact

00:31:45.700 --> 00:31:48.170
that the languages
are kind of varied,

00:31:48.170 --> 00:31:50.530
so that I don't
have to learn go,

00:31:50.530 --> 00:31:54.142
even though everybody loves go.

00:31:54.142 --> 00:31:55.600
It's really great
because it allows

00:31:55.600 --> 00:31:59.320
you to develop in the IDE
of your choice, and in a way

00:31:59.320 --> 00:32:01.390
that you'll be comfortable
basically building

00:32:01.390 --> 00:32:04.620
and leveraging those APIs.

00:32:04.620 --> 00:32:07.170
As mentioned, they take care
of rate limiting for you.

00:32:07.170 --> 00:32:10.740
So there's a default of 50
queries per second built

00:32:10.740 --> 00:32:11.940
into those client libraries.

00:32:11.940 --> 00:32:15.110
But there is a way for
you to set that yourself.

00:32:15.110 --> 00:32:17.370
It will take care of
exponential back off

00:32:17.370 --> 00:32:19.770
and error handling for you.

00:32:19.770 --> 00:32:22.920
It'll handle signing your
request with your API key

00:32:22.920 --> 00:32:27.410
so that it's not in
your client code.

00:32:27.410 --> 00:32:32.000
POJO, so basically when you
make a request to the Maps APIs,

00:32:32.000 --> 00:32:35.460
you get returned an
XML or a JSON object.

00:32:35.460 --> 00:32:38.190
But using the Java
client library,

00:32:38.190 --> 00:32:42.450
it'll actually translate
those into usable objects.

00:32:42.450 --> 00:32:45.660
And the client library
support, both asynchronous

00:32:45.660 --> 00:32:50.760
and asynchronous API calling,
depending on your needs.

00:32:50.760 --> 00:32:53.190
Cool, so I haven't been to
the code labs area here yet.

00:32:53.190 --> 00:32:57.150
But there is a code lab
on the client libraries.

00:32:57.150 --> 00:33:00.540
It's actually hosted
by Brett Morgan,

00:33:00.540 --> 00:33:01.830
whose team built the library.

00:33:01.830 --> 00:33:03.872
So very knowledgeable.

00:33:03.872 --> 00:33:06.330
Like I said, you can leave here
and you can in five minutes

00:33:06.330 --> 00:33:08.430
start using the APIs.

00:33:08.430 --> 00:33:10.194
Probably worth it to
go to this code lab

00:33:10.194 --> 00:33:11.610
if you're interested
in doing that

00:33:11.610 --> 00:33:14.550
and get some hands on help
with building these server side

00:33:14.550 --> 00:33:17.224
applications using
those cloud libraries.

00:33:17.224 --> 00:33:19.140
And if you don't do it,
it's available online.

00:33:19.140 --> 00:33:21.002
You can do it at home.

00:33:21.002 --> 00:33:22.335
These code labs really valuable.

00:33:25.280 --> 00:33:28.370
So we've gone over
a lot of stuff.

00:33:28.370 --> 00:33:30.320
Pretty nitty gritty
stuff at some points.

00:33:30.320 --> 00:33:33.670
But you're probably at
this point like great.

00:33:33.670 --> 00:33:36.170
Where can I find all
this stuff online?

00:33:36.170 --> 00:33:38.350
You can.

00:33:38.350 --> 00:33:40.520
The geo developer's log
is a really great way

00:33:40.520 --> 00:33:43.510
to stay up to date with
these specific things.

00:33:43.510 --> 00:33:45.960
The client libraries, I
think are relatively new.

00:33:45.960 --> 00:33:48.626
But there would have been a blog
post telling you all about them

00:33:48.626 --> 00:33:49.880
and how great they are.

00:33:49.880 --> 00:33:52.820
We have across the
maps API organization,

00:33:52.820 --> 00:33:54.830
people contribute to this blog.

00:33:54.830 --> 00:33:58.990
And so it's just a really
great place to keep in touch.

00:33:58.990 --> 00:34:02.920
Our developer site is
also a really great place

00:34:02.920 --> 00:34:06.757
to get technical
documentation, sample code.

00:34:06.757 --> 00:34:08.590
You can probably build
an entire application

00:34:08.590 --> 00:34:11.006
just by copying and pasting
stuff from the developer site.

00:34:11.006 --> 00:34:14.260
(WHISPERS) That's what I do.

00:34:14.260 --> 00:34:15.790
Like I mentioned,
so the home page

00:34:15.790 --> 00:34:19.699
will give you latest
updates, videos, tutorials.

00:34:19.699 --> 00:34:21.699
That video on the geo
coding API error handling

00:34:21.699 --> 00:34:23.170
was on the developer site.

00:34:23.170 --> 00:34:25.570
This is just the
default place that you

00:34:25.570 --> 00:34:28.360
should go to find out
information about the Google

00:34:28.360 --> 00:34:31.239
Maps APIs.

00:34:31.239 --> 00:34:32.170
The beta program.

00:34:32.170 --> 00:34:34.690
So this is another thing
that is relatively new,

00:34:34.690 --> 00:34:38.110
but a really cool way
to be the first person

00:34:38.110 --> 00:34:40.480
to be using certain features.

00:34:40.480 --> 00:34:42.250
And you can actually
not only use

00:34:42.250 --> 00:34:44.320
new features in
the beta program,

00:34:44.320 --> 00:34:49.909
you can directly influence
how we build the APIs.

00:34:49.909 --> 00:34:54.050
So this is another nerd moment
for me, but I work at Google.

00:34:54.050 --> 00:34:55.360
So that's not a good example.

00:34:55.360 --> 00:34:57.160
I can influence stuff I guess.

00:34:57.160 --> 00:35:00.430
But if I did, it's
really cool to be

00:35:00.430 --> 00:35:02.890
able to just be
like I contributed

00:35:02.890 --> 00:35:04.720
to this new awesome API.

00:35:04.720 --> 00:35:08.200
This is my feedback that
has resulted in this result,

00:35:08.200 --> 00:35:11.421
this feature that
I'm able to use now.

00:35:11.421 --> 00:35:13.920
It's a really great way to just
be really directly connected

00:35:13.920 --> 00:35:16.761
with us.

00:35:16.761 --> 00:35:17.260
Awesome.

00:35:17.260 --> 00:35:19.960
So I leave you with that.

00:35:19.960 --> 00:35:22.480
Get excited about
using location data.

00:35:22.480 --> 00:35:24.100
It's very easy to do.

00:35:24.100 --> 00:35:26.620
There are a million
possibilities of the ways

00:35:26.620 --> 00:35:29.140
that you can use it to
enrich your business.

00:35:29.140 --> 00:35:33.085
And you can get started today.

00:35:33.085 --> 00:35:33.630
Awesome.

